---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify: Which Fits Your Store?';
const description = 'A focused comparison of Brevo and Mailchimp for Shopify email, automation, pricing, consent, and operating fit.';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Which is easier to operate?', a: 'Mailchimp is often easier for a familiar email-first team, while Brevo can be attractive when send-volume economics and broader messaging options matter. Test the actual Shopify workflows rather than relying on a demo.' },
  { q: 'How should Shopify pricing be compared?', a: 'Model contacts, sends, SMS, automation, seats, integrations, support, and seasonal volume for twelve months. The cheapest entry plan is not necessarily the cheapest operating system.' },
  { q: 'Can either tool handle cart recovery?', a: 'Both can support cart workflows, but the useful comparison is trigger freshness, purchase suppression, product data, consent, reporting, and time for the team to maintain the flow.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="py-12 md:py-20"><div class="container max-w-4xl">
    <a href="/compare" class="text-sm text-muted inline-block mb-6">← All comparisons</a>
    <header class="mb-10"><p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary mb-3">Two-product Shopify comparison</p><h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">{title}</h1><p class="text-xl text-muted">Brevo and Mailchimp can both support Shopify email marketing, but their pricing models, channel breadth, and operating feel differ. This page compares only those two products.</p></header>
    <section class="rounded-xl border border-primary/20 p-6 mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Short answer</h2><p>Choose Brevo when send-volume economics, transactional coverage, and broader messaging options matter to a cost-conscious team. Choose Mailchimp when email campaigns, creative familiarity, and a straightforward audience workflow are the priority.</p><p class="mt-3">For either platform, Shopify quality depends on event freshness and suppression. Test welcome, cart, post-purchase, and win-back with purchases, refunds, consent changes, and duplicate events before judging the dashboard.</p></section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Decision snapshot</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Brevo</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best fit</td><td>Cost-aware sending and email plus transactional coverage</td><td>Familiar email campaigns and audience workflows</td></tr><tr><td>Core strength</td><td>Send-volume flexibility and broad messaging options</td><td>Creative workflow, templates, and approachable campaign operations</td></tr><tr><td>Main trade-off</td><td>Shopify-specific depth and automation may require validation</td><td>Contact pricing and ecommerce complexity need careful modeling</td></tr><tr><td>Pricing question</td><td>How do sends, contacts, SMS, seats, and automation scale?</td><td>How do contacts, sends, features, and add-ons scale?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Brevo: when it fits</h2><p>Brevo is worth evaluating when the store wants dependable email volume, transactional messaging, and a cost model that is not driven only by contact count. It can suit a lean operator who needs campaigns and service communication in a broader messaging workspace.</p><p class="mt-3">The diligence question is Shopify event depth. Verify customer and product synchronization, cart and purchase triggers, refund handling, consent fields, template ownership, and the separation between service and promotion. Model implementation and troubleshooting time alongside the plan.</p>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Mailchimp: when it fits</h2><p>Mailchimp remains practical for teams that value a familiar email editor, campaign production, newsletters, and accessible audience operations. It can be a sensible choice when Shopify automation is important but not yet the store’s most complex data problem.</p><p class="mt-3">Test product blocks, purchase suppression, audience updates, unsubscribe behavior, and reporting with real Shopify records. Include contact growth, seasonal sends, SMS credits, user seats, and the labor required to keep segments from becoming stale.</p>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Controlled Shopify pilot</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Brevo</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry</td><td>Shopify customer, event, segment, or transactional state</td><td>Shopify customer, audience, product, or campaign event</td></tr><tr><td>Exit</td><td>Purchase, refund, opt-out, reply, or service handoff</td><td>Purchase, unsubscribe, audience change, or journey goal</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>Stale event, wrong contact identity, duplicate send, or plan threshold</td><td>Stale audience, duplicate message, missing product event, or cost jump</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Incremental conversion, delivery quality, and manageable spend</td><td>Conversion, repeat value, creative throughput, and manageable spend</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Final verdict</h2><p>Brevo wins when send economics and broader messaging coverage are central. Mailchimp wins when email-first familiarity and campaign production matter more. Choose after both products pass the same Shopify pilot and the twelve-month model includes contacts, sends, channels, support, and operator time.</p><p class="mt-6">Continue with the <a href="/blog">Shopify email guides</a>, <a href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase playbook</a>, and <a href="/alternatives">alternatives library</a>.</p>
    <section class="mt-12"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">FAQ</h2>{faqs.map(f=><details class="border-b border-border py-4"><summary class="font-semibold">{f.q}</summary><p class="mt-3 text-muted">{f.a}</p></details>)}</section>
  </div></article>
</BaseLayout>
